[2026-03-14T22:23:30.169Z] {"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01S38A6p2JnLtQE5bnUsEuHz","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→156-- **Initial state**: User is authenticated, workspace has teams \"Engineering\" and \"Design\"\n     2→157:- **Expected**: The sidebar displays a \"Team Section\" with each team name as a collapsible section header. Under each team: \"Issues\" (list icon), \"Active Cycle\" (refresh-cw icon), \"Projects\" (folder icon), \"Views\" (layout icon).\n     3→158-\n     4→--\n     5→161-- **Action**: User clicks the \"Engineering\" team section header\n     6→162:- **Expected**: The team section collapses, hiding Issues, Active Cycle, Projects, and Views sub-links. Clicking again expands it back.\n     7→163-\n     8→--\n     9→173-\n    10→174:#### Test: Sidebar team Active Cycle link navigates to cycles page\n    11→175-- **Initial state**: User is authenticated, \"Engineering\" team has id \"eng-1\"\n    12→176:- **Action**: User clicks \"Active Cycle\" under the \"Engineering\" team section\n    13→177:- **Expected**: User is navigated to `/team/eng-1/cycles`.\n    14→178-\n    15→--\n    16→274-- **Initial state**: User is authenticated, sidebar is in icon-only mode, workspace has team \"Engineering\"\n    17→275:- **Expected**: Team navigation items are still accessible in icon-only mode (icons for Issues, Active Cycle, Projects, Views are visible and clickable).\n    18→276-\n    19→--\n    20→838-- **Initial state**: User is authenticated, viewing `/team/eng-1/issues`\n    21→839:- **Expected**: A toolbar at the top of the issue list displays filter controls: \"Status\", \"Priority\", \"Assignee\", \"Label\", \"Project\", and \"Cycle\" filters. Each shows as a button/chip that can be clicked to open a dropdown.\n    22→840-\n    23→--\n    24→890-\n    25→891:#### Test: Cycle filter dropdown shows team cycles\n    26→892:- **Initial state**: User is authenticated, team has cycles \"Sprint 1\" (active), \"Sprint 2\" (upcoming)\n    27→893:- **Action**: User clicks the \"Cycle\" filter button\n    28→894:- **Expected**: A multi-select dropdown opens showing \"Sprint 1\", \"Sprint 2\", and a \"No Cycle\" option. The active cycle is visually indicated. Each option has a checkbox.\n    29→895-\n    30→896:#### Test: Cycle filter filters issues by selected cycle\n    31→897:- **Initial state**: User is authenticated, team has issues: ENG-1 (in \"Sprint 1\"), ENG-2 (in \"Sprint 1\"), ENG-3 (no cycle)\n    32→898:- **Action**: User opens Cycle filter and selects \"Sprint 1\"\n    33→899-- **Expected**: Only issues in Sprint 1 are shown (ENG-1 and ENG-2). ENG-3 is hidden.\n    34→--\n    35→1114-#### Test: Sidebar renders all property fields\n    36→1115:- **Initial state**: User is on the issue detail page for an issue with status \"In Progress\", priority \"High\", assignee \"Alice\", labels [\"Bug\", \"Frontend\"], project \"Auth Rewrite\", cycle \"Sprint 3\", due date \"2026-04-01\"\n    37→1116:- **Expected**: The right sidebar displays all property fields: Status showing \"In Progress\" with half-circle yellow icon, Priority showing \"High\" with orange arrow-up icon, Assignee showing \"Alice\" with avatar, Labels showing \"Bug\" and \"Frontend\" as colored badges, Project showing \"Auth Rewrite\", Cycle showing \"Sprint 3\", Due Date showing \"Apr 1, 2026\", Created timestamp (read-only), and Updated timestamp (read-only).\n    38→1117-\n    39→--\n    40→1184-\n    41→1185:#### Test: Change cycle via dropdown\n    42→1186:- **Initial state**: Issue belongs to team \"Engineering\" which has cycles \"Sprint 2\" (completed), \"Sprint 3\" (active), \"Sprint 4\" (upcoming). Issue has no cycle assigned.\n    43→1187:- **Action**: User clicks the Cycle field in the sidebar\n    44→1188:- **Expected**: A dropdown appears showing available cycles for the team: \"Sprint 2\", \"Sprint 3\", \"Sprint 4\" with their date ranges.\n    45→1189-\n    46→1190:#### Test: Select cycle\n    47→1191:- **Initial state**: The cycle dropdown is open\n    48→1192-- **Action**: User clicks \"Sprint 3\"\n    49→1193:- **Expected**: The dropdown closes. Cycle updates to show \"Sprint 3\". The change is persisted. An activity entry is created.\n    50→1194-\n    51→--\n    52→1231-- **Initial state**: User is on Team Issues page for team \"Engineering\"\n    53→1232:- **Expected**: Modal displays: Team selector dropdown, Title text input, Description text area, Status dropdown, Priority dropdown, Assignee searchable selector, Labels multi-select, Project searchable selector, Cycle dropdown, Due date picker, and Parent issue searchable selector. All fields are visible and properly labeled.\n    54→1233-\n    55→--\n    56→1250-- **Action**: User clicks the Team selector dropdown and selects \"Design\"\n    57→1251:- **Expected**: Team selector now shows \"Design\". The Cycle dropdown updates to show \"Design\" team's cycles instead of \"Engineering\" team's cycles.\n    58→1252-\n    59→--\n    60→1325-\n    61→1326:#### Test: Cycle dropdown shows team's available cycles\n    62→1327:- **Initial state**: Create issue modal is open with team \"Engineering\" selected. Team has cycles \"Sprint 10\" (active) and \"Sprint 11\" (upcoming).\n    63→1328:- **Action**: User clicks the Cycle dropdown\n    64→1329:- **Expected**: Dropdown shows \"Sprint 10\" and \"Sprint 11\". The active cycle is indicated.\n    65→1330-\n    66→1331:#### Test: Cycle dropdown allows selecting a cycle\n    67→1332:- **Initial state**: Create issue modal is open, Cycle field is empty\n    68→1333:- **Action**: User clicks the Cycle dropdown and selects \"Sprint 10\"\n    69→1334:- **Expected**: Cycle field shows \"Sprint 10\".\n    70→1335-\n    71→1336:#### Test: Cycle dropdown updates when team is changed\n    72→1337:- **Initial state**: Create issue modal is open with team \"Engineering\" selected showing its cycles\n    73→1338-- **Action**: User changes team to \"Design\" via the Team selector\n    74→1339:- **Expected**: The Cycle dropdown clears its selection and now shows \"Design\" team's cycles instead of \"Engineering\" cycles.\n    75→1340-\n    76→--\n    77→1396-#### Test: Create Issue button submits the form with all fields\n    78→1397:- **Initial state**: Create issue modal is open. User has filled in: Title \"Implement OAuth\", Description \"Add OAuth support\", Status \"Todo\", Priority \"High\", Assignee \"Alice Smith\", Labels \"Feature\", Project \"API Redesign\", Cycle \"Sprint 10\", Due date \"Mar 25, 2026\".\n    79→1398-- **Action**: User clicks \"Create Issue\"\n    80→--\n    81→1403-- **Action**: User enters title \"Quick bug fix\" and clicks \"Create Issue\"\n    82→1404:- **Expected**: The issue is created with title \"Quick bug fix\", status \"Backlog\", priority \"No Priority\", and no assignee/labels/project/cycle/due date. The new issue identifier is briefly shown. The modal closes.\n    83→1405-\n    84→--\n    85→1445-\n    86→1446:## Active Cycle Page (`/team/:teamId/cycles`)\n    87→1447-\n    88→1448:**Components**: CycleList, CycleDetail, CreateCycle, BurndownChart\n    89→1449-\n    90→1450:### CycleList\n    91→1451-\n    92→1452:#### Test: Cycle list renders all team cycles with name, date range, progress bar, and issue count\n    93→1453:- **Initial state**: User is authenticated, navigates to `/team/eng-1/cycles`. The \"Engineering\" team has 3 cycles: \"Sprint 1\" (Jan 1–Jan 14, 8/10 issues done), \"Sprint 2\" (Jan 15–Jan 28, 3/8 issues done, active), \"Sprint 3\" (Jan 29–Feb 11, 0/5 issues done).\n    94→1454:- **Expected**: A list of 3 cycle rows is displayed. Each row shows the cycle name, date range (e.g., \"Jan 1 – Jan 14\"), a progress bar reflecting the completion percentage (e.g., 80% for Sprint 1), and the issue count (e.g., \"10 issues\"). Rows are ordered chronologically.\n    95→1455-\n    96→1456:#### Test: Active cycle is visually highlighted in the list\n    97→1457:- **Initial state**: User is on `/team/eng-1/cycles`. \"Sprint 2\" is the active cycle.\n    98→1458:- **Expected**: The \"Sprint 2\" row has a distinct visual highlight (e.g., accent border, background color, or \"Active\" badge) that distinguishes it from the other cycles. Non-active cycles do not have this highlight.\n    99→1459-\n   100→1460:#### Test: Clicking a cycle navigates to cycle detail view\n   101→1461:- **Initial state**: User is on `/team/eng-1/cycles` viewing the cycle list.\n   102→1462:- **Action**: User clicks on the \"Sprint 2\" cycle row.\n   103→1463:- **Expected**: The cycle detail view for \"Sprint 2\" is displayed, showing the cycle name, date range, progress stats, issue list, and burndown chart.\n   104→1464-\n   105→1465:#### Test: Cycle list shows empty state when team has no cycles\n   106→1466:- **Initial state**: User is authenticated, navigates to `/team/design-1/cycles`. The \"Design\" team has no cycles.\n   107→1467:- **Expected**: An empty state message is displayed (e.g., \"No cycles yet. Create your first cycle to get started.\") with an icon. The \"New Cycle\" button is still visible.\n   108→1468-\n   109→1469:#### Test: Cycle list progress bar accurately reflects issue completion percentage\n   110→1470:- **Initial state**: User is on `/team/eng-1/cycles`. \"Sprint 1\" has 8 of 10 issues done (80%), \"Sprint 2\" has 3 of 8 done (37.5%), \"Sprint 3\" has 0 of 5 done (0%).\n   111→1471:- **Expected**: Each cycle's progress bar width corresponds to its completion percentage. Sprint 1 shows ~80% filled, Sprint 2 shows ~37.5% filled, Sprint 3 shows 0% filled (empty bar).\n   112→1472-\n   113→1473:#### Test: Cycle list updates when a new cycle is created\n   114→1474:- **Initial state**: User is on `/team/eng-1/cycles` with 2 existing cycles.\n   115→1475:- **Action**: User creates a new cycle \"Sprint 4\" via the \"New Cycle\" button.\n   116→1476:- **Expected**: The cycle list now shows 3 cycles, with \"Sprint 4\" appearing in the list with 0 issues and an empty progress bar.\n   117→1477-\n   118→1478:#### Test: Clicking a different cycle after viewing one switches the detail view\n   119→1479:- **Initial state**: User is on `/team/eng-1/cycles` and has clicked \"Sprint 2\" to view its detail.\n   120→1480:- **Action**: User clicks back to the cycle list and clicks \"Sprint 1\".\n   121→1481:- **Expected**: The detail view updates to show \"Sprint 1\" cycle information (name, date range, stats, issues, burndown chart). No stale data from \"Sprint 2\" is shown.\n   122→1482-\n   123→1483:### CycleDetail\n   124→1484-\n   125→1485:#### Test: Cycle detail view renders cycle name and date range\n   126→1486:- **Initial state**: User is on `/team/eng-1/cycles` and clicks on \"Sprint 2\" (Jan 15–Jan 28).\n   127→1487:- **Expected**: The detail view header displays the cycle name \"Sprint 2\" and the date range \"Jan 15 – Jan 28\" prominently at the top.\n   128→1488-\n   129→1489:#### Test: Cycle detail shows progress stats (total, completed, in progress, remaining)\n   130→1490:- **Initial state**: User views cycle detail for \"Sprint 2\" which has 8 total issues: 3 done, 2 in progress, 3 remaining (backlog/todo).\n   131→1491-- **Expected**: Four progress stat cards/sections are displayed: \"Total: 8\", \"Completed: 3\", \"In Progress: 2\", \"Remaining: 3\". The stats are clearly labeled and visually distinct.\n   132→1492-\n   133→1493:#### Test: Cycle detail shows progress bar with correct completion percentage\n   134→1494:- **Initial state**: User views cycle detail for \"Sprint 2\" with 3 of 8 issues completed (37.5%).\n   135→1495-- **Expected**: A progress bar is displayed showing approximately 37.5% completion. The percentage text \"37.5%\" (or \"3 of 8\") is visible near the progress bar.\n   136→1496-\n   137→1497:#### Test: Cycle detail displays filtered issue list matching Team Issues layout\n   138→1498:- **Initial state**: User views cycle detail for \"Sprint 2\" which has 8 issues across various statuses and priorities.\n   139→1499-- **Expected**: An issue list is displayed below the progress stats using the same layout as the Team Issues page. Each issue row shows: priority icon, issue identifier (e.g., \"ENG-42\"), title (clickable), status icon, labels as colored badges, assignee avatar, due date, and project name. Issues are grouped by status by default.\n   140→1500-\n   141→1501:#### Test: Clicking an issue in cycle detail navigates to issue detail page\n   142→1502:- **Initial state**: User is viewing cycle detail for \"Sprint 2\" with issue \"ENG-42\" visible.\n   143→1503-- **Action**: User clicks on the issue title \"ENG-42\".\n   144→--\n   145→1505-\n   146→1506:#### Test: Cycle detail issue list supports grouping by status, priority, assignee\n   147→1507:- **Initial state**: User is viewing cycle detail for \"Sprint 2\" with 8 issues.\n   148→1508-- **Action**: User changes the \"Group by\" option from \"Status\" to \"Priority\".\n   149→--\n   150→1510-\n   151→1511:#### Test: Cycle detail issue list supports sorting\n   152→1512:- **Initial state**: User is viewing cycle detail for \"Sprint 2\" with issues grouped by status.\n   153→1513-- **Action**: User changes \"Sort by\" to \"Priority\".\n   154→--\n   155→1515-\n   156→1516:#### Test: Cycle detail issue list supports filtering\n   157→1517:- **Initial state**: User is viewing cycle detail for \"Sprint 2\" with 8 issues. 2 issues have status \"Done\".\n   158→1518-- **Action**: User opens the Status filter and selects \"Done\" only.\n   159→--\n   160→1520-\n   161→1521:#### Test: Cycle detail issue list supports bulk actions\n   162→1522:- **Initial state**: User is viewing cycle detail for \"Sprint 2\" with multiple issues.\n   163→1523-- **Action**: User selects checkboxes on 3 issues, then uses the bulk action to change status to \"In Progress\".\n   164→--\n   165→1525-\n   166→1526:#### Test: Cycle detail progress stats update when an issue status changes\n   167→1527:- **Initial state**: User is viewing cycle detail for \"Sprint 2\" with 3 completed and 5 remaining issues.\n   168→1528-- **Action**: User changes one issue's status from \"Todo\" to \"Done\" via the status icon in the issue row.\n   169→--\n   170→1530-\n   171→1531:#### Test: Cycle detail renders correctly for a cycle with no issues\n   172→1532:- **Initial state**: User views a newly created cycle \"Sprint 4\" that has no issues.\n   173→1533:- **Expected**: The progress stats show \"Total: 0\", \"Completed: 0\", \"In Progress: 0\", \"Remaining: 0\". The progress bar is empty (0%). The issue list shows an empty state message (e.g., \"No issues in this cycle\"). The burndown chart shows no data.\n   174→1534-\n   175→1535:### CreateCycle\n   176→1536-\n   177→1537:#### Test: \"New Cycle\" button is visible on the cycles page\n   178→1538:- **Initial state**: User is authenticated, navigates to `/team/eng-1/cycles`.\n   179→1539:- **Expected**: A \"New Cycle\" button is visible on the page, styled consistently with other create buttons in the app (e.g., primary accent color, plus icon or text label \"New Cycle\").\n   180→1540-\n   181→1541:#### Test: Clicking \"New Cycle\" opens create cycle modal with correct fields\n   182→1542:- **Initial state**: User is on `/team/eng-1/cycles`.\n   183→1543:- **Action**: User clicks the \"New Cycle\" button.\n   184→1544:- **Expected**: A modal dialog opens with: a \"Name\" text input field (required), a \"Start date\" date picker, an \"End date\" date picker, a \"Create\" button, and a \"Cancel\" button. The modal has a title like \"New Cycle\" or \"Create Cycle\".\n   185→1545-\n   186→1546:#### Test: Successfully creating a cycle with all fields filled\n   187→1547:- **Initial state**: Create cycle modal is open. Team has no active cycle.\n   188→1548-- **Action**: User enters \"Sprint 5\" in the Name field, selects Feb 12 as Start date, selects Feb 25 as End date, and clicks \"Create\".\n   189→1549:- **Expected**: The modal closes. The cycle list now includes \"Sprint 5\" with the date range \"Feb 12 – Feb 25\", 0 issues, and an empty progress bar. The new cycle appears in the list.\n   190→1550-\n   191→1551:#### Test: Create cycle validates that name is required\n   192→1552:- **Initial state**: Create cycle modal is open.\n   193→1553-- **Action**: User leaves the Name field empty, sets start and end dates, and clicks \"Create\".\n   194→1554:- **Expected**: A validation error message appears near the Name field (e.g., \"Name is required\"). The modal remains open. No cycle is created.\n   195→1555-\n   196→1556:#### Test: Create cycle validates that end date is after start date\n   197→1557:- **Initial state**: Create cycle modal is open.\n   198→1558-- **Action**: User enters \"Sprint 5\" in Name, selects Feb 25 as Start date, selects Feb 12 as End date (before start), and clicks \"Create\".\n   199→1559:- **Expected**: A validation error message appears (e.g., \"End date must be after start date\"). The modal remains open. No cycle is created.\n   200→1560-\n   201→1561:#### Test: Create cycle validates start and end dates are required\n   202→1562:- **Initial state**: Create cycle modal is open.\n   203→1563-- **Action**: User enters \"Sprint 5\" in Name but does not set start or end dates, and clicks \"Create\".\n   204→1564:- **Expected**: Validation error messages appear for both date fields (e.g., \"Start date is required\", \"End date is required\"). The modal remains open. No cycle is created.\n   205→1565-\n   206→1566:#### Test: Cancel button closes the create cycle modal without creating\n   207→1567:- **Initial state**: Create cycle modal is open. User has entered \"Sprint 5\" in the Name field.\n   208→1568-- **Action**: User clicks \"Cancel\".\n   209→1569:- **Expected**: The modal closes. No new cycle is added to the cycle list. The form data is discarded.\n   210→1570-\n   211→1571:#### Test: Only one cycle can be active at a time per team\n   212→1572:- **Initial state**: Team \"Engineering\" already has an active cycle \"Sprint 2\" (Jan 15–Jan 28). Create cycle modal is open.\n   213→1573:- **Action**: User creates a new cycle \"Sprint 5\" with dates overlapping or within the active period.\n   214→1574:- **Expected**: Either the system prevents setting the new cycle as active while another is active (showing an appropriate message), or the new cycle is created as inactive. Only one cycle at a time has the \"active\" highlight in the cycle list.\n   215→1575-\n   216→1576:#### Test: Creating a cycle persists after page refresh\n   217→1577:- **Initial state**: User has just created \"Sprint 5\" via the create cycle modal.\n   218→1578-- **Action**: User refreshes the page.\n   219→1579:- **Expected**: The cycle list still shows \"Sprint 5\" with the correct name, date range, and issue count of 0. The cycle was persisted to the database.\n   220→1580-\n   221→1581:#### Test: Create cycle modal can be opened, cancelled, and reopened\n   222→1582:- **Initial state**: User is on `/team/eng-1/cycles`.\n   223→1583:- **Action**: User clicks \"New Cycle\", enters \"Sprint X\" in the Name field, clicks \"Cancel\". Then user clicks \"New Cycle\" again.\n   224→1584:- **Expected**: The modal opens fresh with empty fields on the second open. No data from the previous attempt is retained. The modal functions correctly for creating a new cycle.\n   225→1585-\n   226→--\n   227→1588-#### Test: Burndown chart renders as a bar chart showing issues completed per day\n   228→1589:- **Initial state**: User views cycle detail for \"Sprint 2\" (Jan 15–Jan 28). Issues were completed on various days: Jan 15 (1 issue), Jan 17 (2 issues), Jan 20 (1 issue).\n   229→1590:- **Expected**: A bar chart is displayed with the x-axis showing dates within the cycle range (Jan 15–Jan 28) and the y-axis showing the number of issues completed. Bars appear for Jan 15 (height 1), Jan 17 (height 2), Jan 20 (height 1). Days with no completions show no bar or a zero-height bar.\n   230→1591-\n   231→1592:#### Test: Burndown chart shows correct date range matching the cycle\n   232→1593:- **Initial state**: User views cycle detail for \"Sprint 2\" (Jan 15–Jan 28).\n   233→1594:- **Expected**: The chart x-axis spans exactly from Jan 15 to Jan 28, matching the cycle's start and end dates. No dates outside this range are shown.\n   234→1595-\n   235→1596-#### Test: Burndown chart updates when an issue is marked as done\n   236→1597:- **Initial state**: User is viewing cycle detail for \"Sprint 2\". The burndown chart shows 3 issues completed so far. Today is Jan 22.\n   237→1598-- **Action**: User changes an issue's status from \"In Progress\" to \"Done\".\n   238→--\n   239→1600-\n   240→1601:#### Test: Burndown chart shows empty state for cycle with no completed issues\n   241→1602:- **Initial state**: User views cycle detail for a new cycle \"Sprint 4\" with 5 issues, all in \"Todo\" or \"Backlog\" status.\n   242→1603-- **Expected**: The burndown chart renders with the correct date range but all bars at zero height (or an empty chart with a message like \"No issues completed yet\"). The chart axes and labels are still visible.\n   243→--\n   244→1605-#### Test: Burndown chart has readable axis labels and chart title\n   245→1606:- **Initial state**: User views cycle detail for \"Sprint 2\" with completions on multiple days.\n   246→1607-- **Expected**: The chart has a visible title or section heading (e.g., \"Burndown\" or \"Issues Completed per Day\"). The x-axis shows date labels that are readable (not overlapping). The y-axis shows integer values for issue counts. The chart uses appropriate spacing and sizing.\n   247→--\n   248→1609-#### Test: Burndown chart bar heights are proportional to issue counts\n   249→1610:- **Initial state**: User views cycle detail for a cycle where Jan 15 had 1 completion, Jan 16 had 4 completions, and Jan 17 had 2 completions.\n   250→1611-- **Expected**: The bar for Jan 16 is visibly taller than Jan 17, which is visibly taller than Jan 15. The relative heights accurately represent the completion counts (4:2:1 ratio).\n   251→1612-\n   252→1613:#### Test: Burndown chart handles a cycle spanning a long date range\n   253→1614:- **Initial state**: User views cycle detail for a cycle spanning 4 weeks (28 days) with completions spread across different days.\n   254→1615-- **Expected**: The chart renders all 28 days on the x-axis without visual overflow. Date labels may be abbreviated or rotated to fit. All bars are visible and correctly positioned. The chart remains readable and scrollable if necessary.\n   255→--\n   256→2250-#### Test: Teams page renders with list of all teams as cards\n   257→2251:- **Initial state**: Workspace has 3 teams: \"Engineering\" (prefix ENG, 5 members, active cycle \"Sprint 10\"), \"Design\" (prefix DES, 3 members, no active cycle), \"QA\" (prefix QA, 2 members, active cycle \"QA Cycle 4\"). User navigates to `/settings/teams`.\n   258→2252:- **Expected**: The page displays 3 team cards. Each card shows the team name, identifier prefix, member count, and active cycle name (if any). A \"Create Team\" button is visible at the top of the page. The page title reads \"Teams\".\n   259→2253-\n   260→--\n   261→2265-\n   262→2266:#### Test: Team card displays active cycle name when team has an active cycle\n   263→2267:- **Initial state**: Workspace has a team \"Engineering\" with an active cycle named \"Sprint 10\". User is on `/settings/teams`.\n   264→2268:- **Expected**: The team card shows the active cycle name \"Sprint 10\" (e.g., with a cycle icon or label like \"Active: Sprint 10\").\n   265→2269-\n   266→2270:#### Test: Team card displays no cycle indicator when team has no active cycle\n   267→2271:- **Initial state**: Workspace has a team \"Design\" with no active cycle. User is on `/settings/teams`.\n   268→2272:- **Expected**: The team card does not show a cycle name. Either a \"No active cycle\" message is displayed or the cycle section is absent. No error or empty artifact is shown.\n   269→2273-\n   270→--\n   271→2285-- **Action**: User creates a new team \"QA\" via the Create Team modal\n   272→2286:- **Expected**: The teams page now shows 3 team cards, including the newly created \"QA\" team with its identifier prefix, a member count of 0 (or 1 if the creator is auto-added), and no active cycle.\n   273→2287-\n   274→--\n   275→2332-- **Action**: User enters Name \"Quality Assurance\", Identifier prefix \"QA\", Description \"QA team for testing\", and clicks \"Create Team\"\n   276→2333:- **Expected**: The modal closes. The teams page now shows the new \"Quality Assurance\" team card with prefix \"QA\", member count of 0 (or 1), and no active cycle. The team is persisted (reloading shows the team).\n   277→2334-"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7db18531-1ab9-4e3d-8311-bcfa64f59629","uuid":"3a8f9155-8545-47dc-b29e-f7ac14b9bb1f","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/agent/.claude/projects/-repo/7db18531-1ab9-4e3d-8311-bcfa64f59629/tool-results/toolu_01C8Gp4HRWVhVGtcxo9ve7po.txt","content":"156-- **Initial state**: User is authenticated, workspace has teams \"Engineering\" and \"Design\"\n157:- **Expected**: The sidebar displays a \"Team Section\" with each team name as a collapsible section header. Under each team: \"Issues\" (list icon), \"Active Cycle\" (refresh-cw icon), \"Projects\" (folder icon), \"Views\" (layout icon).\n158-\n--\n161-- **Action**: User clicks the \"Engineering\" team section header\n162:- **Expected**: The team section collapses, hiding Issues, Active Cycle, Projects, and Views sub-links. Clicking again expands it back.\n163-\n--\n173-\n174:#### Test: Sidebar team Active Cycle link navigates to cycles page\n175-- **Initial state**: User is authenticated, \"Engineering\" team has id \"eng-1\"\n176:- **Action**: User clicks \"Active Cycle\" under the \"Engineering\" team section\n177:- **Expected**: User is navigated to `/team/eng-1/cycles`.\n178-\n--\n274-- **Initial state**: User is authenticated, sidebar is in icon-only mode, workspace has team \"Engineering\"\n275:- **Expected**: Team navigation items are still accessible in icon-only mode (icons for Issues, Active Cycle, Projects, Views are visible and clickable).\n276-\n--\n838-- **Initial state**: User is authenticated, viewing `/team/eng-1/issues`\n839:- **Expected**: A toolbar at the top of the issue list displays filter controls: \"Status\", \"Priority\", \"Assignee\", \"Label\", \"Project\", and \"Cycle\" filters. Each shows as a button/chip that can be clicked to open a dropdown.\n840-\n--\n890-\n891:#### Test: Cycle filter dropdown shows team cycles\n892:- **Initial state**: User is authenticated, team has cycles \"Sprint 1\" (active), \"Sprint 2\" (upcoming)\n893:- **Action**: User clicks the \"Cycle\" filter button\n894:- **Expected**: A multi-select dropdown opens showing \"Sprint 1\", \"Sprint 2\", and a \"No Cycle\" option. The active cycle is visually indicated. Each option has a checkbox.\n895-\n896:#### Test: Cycle filter filters issues by selected cycle\n897:- **Initial state**: User is authenticated, team has issues: ENG-1 (in \"Sprint 1\"), ENG-2 (in \"Sprint 1\"), ENG-3 (no cycle)\n898:- **Action**: User opens Cycle filter and selects \"Sprint 1\"\n899-- **Expected**: Only issues in Sprint 1 are shown (ENG-1 and ENG-2). ENG-3 is hidden.\n--\n1114-#### Test: Sidebar renders all property fields\n1115:- **Initial state**: User is on the issue detail page for an issue with status \"In Progress\", priority \"High\", assignee \"Alice\", labels [\"Bug\", \"Frontend\"], project \"Auth Rewrite\", cycle \"Sprint 3\", due date \"2026-04-01\"\n1116:- **Expected**: The right sidebar displays all property fields: Status showing \"In Progress\" with half-circle yellow icon, Priority showing \"High\" with orange arrow-up icon, Assignee showing \"Alice\" with avatar, Labels showing \"Bug\" and \"Frontend\" as colored badges, Project showing \"Auth Rewrite\", Cycle showing \"Sprint 3\", Due Date showing \"Apr 1, 2026\", Created timestamp (read-only), and Updated timestamp (read-only).\n1117-\n--\n1184-\n1185:#### Test: Change cycle via dropdown\n1186:- **Initial state**: Issue belongs to team \"Engineering\" which has cycles \"Sprint 2\" (completed), \"Sprint 3\" (active), \"Sprint 4\" (upcoming). Issue has no cycle assigned.\n1187:- **Action**: User clicks the Cycle field in the sidebar\n1188:- **Expected**: A dropdown appears showing available cycles for the team: \"Sprint 2\", \"Sprint 3\", \"Sprint 4\" with their date ranges.\n1189-\n1190:#### Test: Select cycle\n1191:- **Initial state**: The cycle dropdown is open\n1192-- **Action**: User clicks \"Sprint 3\"\n1193:- **Expected**: The dropdown closes. Cycle updates to show \"Sprint 3\". The change is persisted. An activity entry is created.\n1194-\n--\n1231-- **Initial state**: User is on Team Issues page for team \"Engineering\"\n1232:- **Expected**: Modal displays: Team selector dropdown, Title text input, Description text area, Status dropdown, Priority dropdown, Assignee searchable selector, Labels multi-select, Project searchable selector, Cycle dropdown, Due date picker, and Parent issue searchable selector. All fields are visible and properly labeled.\n1233-\n--\n1250-- **Action**: User clicks the Team selector dropdown and selects \"Design\"\n1251:- **Expected**: Team selector now shows \"Design\". The Cycle dropdown updates to show \"Design\" team's cycles instead of \"Engineering\" team's cycles.\n1252-\n--\n1325-\n1326:#### Test: Cycle dropdown shows team's available cycles\n1327:- **Initial state**: Create issue modal is open with team \"Engineering\" selected. Team has cycles \"Sprint 10\" (active) and \"Sprint 11\" (upcoming).\n1328:- **Action**: User clicks the Cycle dropdown\n1329:- **Expected**: Dropdown shows \"Sprint 10\" and \"Sprint 11\". The active cycle is indicated.\n1330-\n1331:#### Test: Cycle dropdown allows selecting a cycle\n1332:- **Initial state**: Create issue modal is open, Cycle field is empty\n1333:- **Action**: User clicks the Cycle dropdown and selects \"Sprint 10\"\n1334:- **Expected**: Cycle field shows \"Sprint 10\".\n1335-\n1336:#### Test: Cycle dropdown updates when team is changed\n1337:- **Initial state**: Create issue modal is open with team \"Engineering\" selected showing its cycles\n1338-- **Action**: User changes team to \"Design\" via the Team selector\n1339:- **Expected**: The Cycle dropdown clears its selection and now shows \"Design\" team's cycles instead of \"Engineering\" cycles.\n1340-\n--\n1396-#### Test: Create Issue button submits the form with all fields\n1397:- **Initial state**: Create issue modal is open. User has filled in: Title \"Implement OAuth\", Description \"Add OAuth support\", Status \"Todo\", Priority \"High\", Assignee \"Alice Smith\", Labels \"Feature\", Project \"API Redesign\", Cycle \"Sprint 10\", Due date \"Mar 25, 2026\".\n1398-- **Action**: User clicks \"Create Issue\"\n--\n1403-- **Action**: User enters title \"Quick bug fix\" and clicks \"Create Issue\"\n1404:- **Expected**: The issue is created with title \"Quick bug fix\", status \"Backlog\", priority \"No Priority\", and no assignee/labels/project/cycle/due date. The new issue identifier is briefly shown. The modal closes.\n1405-\n--\n1445-\n1446:## Active Cycle Page (`/team/:teamId/cycles`)\n1447-\n1448:**Components**: CycleList, CycleDetail, CreateCycle, BurndownChart\n1449-\n1450:### CycleList\n1451-\n1452:#### Test: Cycle list renders all team cycles with name, date range, progress bar, and issue count\n1453:- **Initial state**: User is authenticated, navigates to `/team/eng-1/cycles`. The \"Engineering\" team has 3 cycles: \"Sprint 1\" (Jan 1–Jan 14, 8/10 issues done), \"Sprint 2\" (Jan 15–Jan 28, 3/8 issues done, active), \"Sprint 3\" (Jan 29–Feb 11, 0/5 issues done).\n1454:- **Expected**: A list of 3 cycle rows is displayed. Each row shows the cycle name, date range (e.g., \"Jan 1 – Jan 14\"), a progress bar reflecting the completion percentage (e.g., 80% for Sprint 1), and the issue count (e.g., \"10 issues\"). Rows are ordered chronologically.\n1455-\n1456:#### Test: Active cycle is visually highlighted in the list\n1457:- **Initial state**: User is on `/team/eng-1/cycles`. \"Sprint 2\" is the active cycle.\n1458:- **Expected**: The \"Sprint 2\" row has a distinct visual highlight (e.g., accent border, background color, or \"Active\" badge) that distinguishes it from the other cycles. Non-active cycles do not have this highlight.\n1459-\n1460:#### Test: Clicking a cycle navigates to cycle detail view\n1461:- **Initial state**: User is on `/team/eng-1/cycles` viewing the cycle list.\n1462:- **Action**: User clicks on the \"Sprint 2\" cycle row.\n1463:- **Expected**: The cycle detail view for \"Sprint 2\" is displayed, showing the cycle name, date range, progress stats, issue list, and burndown chart.\n1464-\n1465:#### Test: Cycle list shows empty state when team has no cycles\n1466:- **Initial state**: User is authenticated, navigates to `/team/design-1/cycles`. The \"Design\" team has no cycles.\n1467:- **Expected**: An empty state message is displayed (e.g., \"No cycles yet. Create your first cycle to get started.\") with an icon. The \"New Cycle\" button is still visible.\n1468-\n1469:#### Test: Cycle list progress bar accurately reflects issue completion percentage\n1470:- **Initial state**: User is on `/team/eng-1/cycles`. \"Sprint 1\" has 8 of 10 issues done (80%), \"Sprint 2\" has 3 of 8 done (37.5%), \"Sprint 3\" has 0 of 5 done (0%).\n1471:- **Expected**: Each cycle's progress bar width corresponds to its completion percentage. Sprint 1 shows ~80% filled, Sprint 2 shows ~37.5% filled, Sprint 3 shows 0% filled (empty bar).\n1472-\n1473:#### Test: Cycle list updates when a new cycle is created\n1474:- **Initial state**: User is on `/team/eng-1/cycles` with 2 existing cycles.\n1475:- **Action**: User creates a new cycle \"Sprint 4\" via the \"New Cycle\" button.\n1476:- **Expected**: The cycle list now shows 3 cycles, with \"Sprint 4\" appearing in the list with 0 issues and an empty progress bar.\n1477-\n1478:#### Test: Clicking a different cycle after viewing one switches the detail view\n1479:- **Initial state**: User is on `/team/eng-1/cycles` and has clicked \"Sprint 2\" to view its detail.\n1480:- **Action**: User clicks back to the cycle list and clicks \"Sprint 1\".\n1481:- **Expected**: The detail view updates to show \"Sprint 1\" cycle information (name, date range, stats, issues, burndown chart). No stale data from \"Sprint 2\" is shown.\n1482-\n1483:### CycleDetail\n1484-\n1485:#### Test: Cycle detail view renders cycle name and date range\n1486:- **Initial state**: User is on `/team/eng-1/cycles` and clicks on \"Sprint 2\" (Jan 15–Jan 28).\n1487:- **Expected**: The detail view header displays the cycle name \"Sprint 2\" and the date range \"Jan 15 – Jan 28\" prominently at the top.\n1488-\n1489:#### Test: Cycle detail shows progress stats (total, completed, in progress, remaining)\n1490:- **Initial state**: User views cycle detail for \"Sprint 2\" which has 8 total issues: 3 done, 2 in progress, 3 remaining (backlog/todo).\n1491-- **Expected**: Four progress stat cards/sections are displayed: \"Total: 8\", \"Completed: 3\", \"In Progress: 2\", \"Remaining: 3\". The stats are clearly labeled and visually distinct.\n1492-\n1493:#### Test: Cycle detail shows progress bar with correct completion percentage\n1494:- **Initial state**: User views cycle detail for \"Sprint 2\" with 3 of 8 issues completed (37.5%).\n1495-- **Expected**: A progress bar is displayed showing approximately 37.5% completion. The percentage text \"37.5%\" (or \"3 of 8\") is visible near the progress bar.\n1496-\n1497:#### Test: Cycle detail displays filtered issue list matching Team Issues layout\n1498:- **Initial state**: User views cycle detail for \"Sprint 2\" which has 8 issues across various statuses and priorities.\n1499-- **Expected**: An issue list is displayed below the progress stats using the same layout as the Team Issues page. Each issue row shows: priority icon, issue identifier (e.g., \"ENG-42\"), title (clickable), status icon, labels as colored badges, assignee avatar, due date, and project name. Issues are grouped by status by default.\n1500-\n1501:#### Test: Clicking an issue in cycle detail navigates to issue detail page\n1502:- **Initial state**: User is viewing cycle detail for \"Sprint 2\" with issue \"ENG-42\" visible.\n1503-- **Action**: User clicks on the issue title \"ENG-42\".\n--\n1505-\n1506:#### Test: Cycle detail issue list supports grouping by status, priority, assignee\n1507:- **Initial state**: User is viewing cycle detail for \"Sprint 2\" with 8 issues.\n1508-- **Action**: User changes the \"Group by\" option from \"Status\" to \"Priority\".\n--\n1510-\n1511:#### Test: Cycle detail issue list supports sorting\n1512:- **Initial state**: User is viewing cycle detail for \"Sprint 2\" with issues grouped by status.\n1513-- **Action**: User changes \"Sort by\" to \"Priority\".\n--\n1515-\n1516:#### Test: Cycle detail issue list supports filtering\n1517:- **Initial state**: User is viewing cycle detail for \"Sprint 2\" with 8 issues. 2 issues have status \"Done\".\n1518-- **Action**: User opens the Status filter and selects \"Done\" only.\n--\n1520-\n1521:#### Test: Cycle detail issue list supports bulk actions\n1522:- **Initial state**: User is viewing cycle detail for \"Sprint 2\" with multiple issues.\n1523-- **Action**: User selects checkboxes on 3 issues, then uses the bulk action to change status to \"In Progress\".\n--\n1525-\n1526:#### Test: Cycle detail progress stats update when an issue status changes\n1527:- **Initial state**: User is viewing cycle detail for \"Sprint 2\" with 3 completed and 5 remaining issues.\n1528-- **Action**: User changes one issue's status from \"Todo\" to \"Done\" via the status icon in the issue row.\n--\n1530-\n1531:#### Test: Cycle detail renders correctly for a cycle with no issues\n1532:- **Initial state**: User views a newly created cycle \"Sprint 4\" that has no issues.\n1533:- **Expected**: The progress stats show \"Total: 0\", \"Completed: 0\", \"In Progress: 0\", \"Remaining: 0\". The progress bar is empty (0%). The issue list shows an empty state message (e.g., \"No issues in this cycle\"). The burndown chart shows no data.\n1534-\n1535:### CreateCycle\n1536-\n1537:#### Test: \"New Cycle\" button is visible on the cycles page\n1538:- **Initial state**: User is authenticated, navigates to `/team/eng-1/cycles`.\n1539:- **Expected**: A \"New Cycle\" button is visible on the page, styled consistently with other create buttons in the app (e.g., primary accent color, plus icon or text label \"New Cycle\").\n1540-\n1541:#### Test: Clicking \"New Cycle\" opens create cycle modal with correct fields\n1542:- **Initial state**: User is on `/team/eng-1/cycles`.\n1543:- **Action**: User clicks the \"New Cycle\" button.\n1544:- **Expected**: A modal dialog opens with: a \"Name\" text input field (required), a \"Start date\" date picker, an \"End date\" date picker, a \"Create\" button, and a \"Cancel\" button. The modal has a title like \"New Cycle\" or \"Create Cycle\".\n1545-\n1546:#### Test: Successfully creating a cycle with all fields filled\n1547:- **Initial state**: Create cycle modal is open. Team has no active cycle.\n1548-- **Action**: User enters \"Sprint 5\" in the Name field, selects Feb 12 as Start date, selects Feb 25 as End date, and clicks \"Create\".\n1549:- **Expected**: The modal closes. The cycle list now includes \"Sprint 5\" with the date range \"Feb 12 – Feb 25\", 0 issues, and an empty progress bar. The new cycle appears in the list.\n1550-\n1551:#### Test: Create cycle validates that name is required\n1552:- **Initial state**: Create cycle modal is open.\n1553-- **Action**: User leaves the Name field empty, sets start and end dates, and clicks \"Create\".\n1554:- **Expected**: A validation error message appears near the Name field (e.g., \"Name is required\"). The modal remains open. No cycle is created.\n1555-\n1556:#### Test: Create cycle validates that end date is after start date\n1557:- **Initial state**: Create cycle modal is open.\n1558-- **Action**: User enters \"Sprint 5\" in Name, selects Feb 25 as Start date, selects Feb 12 as End date (before start), and clicks \"Create\".\n1559:- **Expected**: A validation error message appears (e.g., \"End date must be after start date\"). The modal remains open. No cycle is created.\n1560-\n1561:#### Test: Create cycle validates start and end dates are required\n1562:- **Initial state**: Create cycle modal is open.\n1563-- **Action**: User enters \"Sprint 5\" in Name but does not set start or end dates, and clicks \"Create\".\n1564:- **Expected**: Validation error messages appear for both date fields (e.g., \"Start date is required\", \"End date is required\"). The modal remains open. No cycle is created.\n1565-\n1566:#### Test: Cancel button closes the create cycle modal without creating\n1567:- **Initial state**: Create cycle modal is open. User has entered \"Sprint 5\" in the Name field.\n1568-- **Action**: User clicks \"Cancel\".\n1569:- **Expected**: The modal closes. No new cycle is added to the cycle list. The form data is discarded.\n1570-\n1571:#### Test: Only one cycle can be active at a time per team\n1572:- **Initial state**: Team \"Engineering\" already has an active cycle \"Sprint 2\" (Jan 15–Jan 28). Create cycle modal is open.\n1573:- **Action**: User creates a new cycle \"Sprint 5\" with dates overlapping or within the active period.\n1574:- **Expected**: Either the system prevents setting the new cycle as active while another is active (showing an appropriate message), or the new cycle is created as inactive. Only one cycle at a time has the \"active\" highlight in the cycle list.\n1575-\n1576:#### Test: Creating a cycle persists after page refresh\n1577:- **Initial state**: User has just created \"Sprint 5\" via the create cycle modal.\n1578-- **Action**: User refreshes the page.\n1579:- **Expected**: The cycle list still shows \"Sprint 5\" with the correct name, date range, and issue count of 0. The cycle was persisted to the database.\n1580-\n1581:#### Test: Create cycle modal can be opened, cancelled, and reopened\n1582:- **Initial state**: User is on `/team/eng-1/cycles`.\n1583:- **Action**: User clicks \"New Cycle\", enters \"Sprint X\" in the Name field, clicks \"Cancel\". Then user clicks \"New Cycle\" again.\n1584:- **Expected**: The modal opens fresh with empty fields on the second open. No data from the previous attempt is retained. The modal functions correctly for creating a new cycle.\n1585-\n--\n1588-#### Test: Burndown chart renders as a bar chart showing issues completed per day\n1589:- **Initial state**: User views cycle detail for \"Sprint 2\" (Jan 15–Jan 28). Issues were completed on various days: Jan 15 (1 issue), Jan 17 (2 issues), Jan 20 (1 issue).\n1590:- **Expected**: A bar chart is displayed with the x-axis showing dates within the cycle range (Jan 15–Jan 28) and the y-axis showing the number of issues completed. Bars appear for Jan 15 (height 1), Jan 17 (height 2), Jan 20 (height 1). Days with no completions show no bar or a zero-height bar.\n1591-\n1592:#### Test: Burndown chart shows correct date range matching the cycle\n1593:- **Initial state**: User views cycle detail for \"Sprint 2\" (Jan 15–Jan 28).\n1594:- **Expected**: The chart x-axis spans exactly from Jan 15 to Jan 28, matching the cycle's start and end dates. No dates outside this range are shown.\n1595-\n1596-#### Test: Burndown chart updates when an issue is marked as done\n1597:- **Initial state**: User is viewing cycle detail for \"Sprint 2\". The burndown chart shows 3 issues completed so far. Today is Jan 22.\n1598-- **Action**: User changes an issue's status from \"In Progress\" to \"Done\".\n--\n1600-\n1601:#### Test: Burndown chart shows empty state for cycle with no completed issues\n1602:- **Initial state**: User views cycle detail for a new cycle \"Sprint 4\" with 5 issues, all in \"Todo\" or \"Backlog\" status.\n1603-- **Expected**: The burndown chart renders with the correct date range but all bars at zero height (or an empty chart with a message like \"No issues completed yet\"). The chart axes and labels are still visible.\n--\n1605-#### Test: Burndown chart has readable axis labels and chart title\n1606:- **Initial state**: User views cycle detail for \"Sprint 2\" with completions on multiple days.\n1607-- **Expected**: The chart has a visible title or section heading (e.g., \"Burndown\" or \"Issues Completed per Day\"). The x-axis shows date labels that are readable (not overlapping). The y-axis shows integer values for issue counts. The chart uses appropriate spacing and sizing.\n--\n1609-#### Test: Burndown chart bar heights are proportional to issue counts\n1610:- **Initial state**: User views cycle detail for a cycle where Jan 15 had 1 completion, Jan 16 had 4 completions, and Jan 17 had 2 completions.\n1611-- **Expected**: The bar for Jan 16 is visibly taller than Jan 17, which is visibly taller than Jan 15. The relative heights accurately represent the completion counts (4:2:1 ratio).\n1612-\n1613:#### Test: Burndown chart handles a cycle spanning a long date range\n1614:- **Initial state**: User views cycle detail for a cycle spanning 4 weeks (28 days) with completions spread across different days.\n1615-- **Expected**: The chart renders all 28 days on the x-axis without visual overflow. Date labels may be abbreviated or rotated to fit. All bars are visible and correctly positioned. The chart remains readable and scrollable if necessary.\n--\n2250-#### Test: Teams page renders with list of all teams as cards\n2251:- **Initial state**: Workspace has 3 teams: \"Engineering\" (prefix ENG, 5 members, active cycle \"Sprint 10\"), \"Design\" (prefix DES, 3 members, no active cycle), \"QA\" (prefix QA, 2 members, active cycle \"QA Cycle 4\"). User navigates to `/settings/teams`.\n2252:- **Expected**: The page displays 3 team cards. Each card shows the team name, identifier prefix, member count, and active cycle name (if any). A \"Create Team\" button is visible at the top of the page. The page title reads \"Teams\".\n2253-\n--\n2265-\n2266:#### Test: Team card displays active cycle name when team has an active cycle\n2267:- **Initial state**: Workspace has a team \"Engineering\" with an active cycle named \"Sprint 10\". User is on `/settings/teams`.\n2268:- **Expected**: The team card shows the active cycle name \"Sprint 10\" (e.g., with a cycle icon or label like \"Active: Sprint 10\").\n2269-\n2270:#### Test: Team card displays no cycle indicator when team has no active cycle\n2271:- **Initial state**: Workspace has a team \"Design\" with no active cycle. User is on `/settings/teams`.\n2272:- **Expected**: The team card does not show a cycle name. Either a \"No active cycle\" message is displayed or the cycle section is absent. No error or empty artifact is shown.\n2273-\n--\n2285-- **Action**: User creates a new team \"QA\" via the Create Team modal\n2286:- **Expected**: The teams page now shows 3 team cards, including the newly created \"QA\" team with its identifier prefix, a member count of 0 (or 1 if the creator is auto-added), and no active cycle.\n2287-\n--\n2332-- **Action**: User enters Name \"Quality Assurance\", Identifier prefix \"QA\", Description \"QA team for testing\", and clicks \"Create Team\"\n2333:- **Expected**: The modal closes. The teams page now shows the new \"Quality Assurance\" team card with prefix \"QA\", member count of 0 (or 1), and no active cycle. The team is persisted (reloading shows the team).\n2334-","numLines":277,"startLine":1,"totalLines":277}}}
